  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 955 PM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nCOOLER AND WETTER WEEKEND: \n\nA substantial pattern change is expected to take place Saturday as \nthe high pressure previously centered over the Four Corners begins \nto break down as a result of an incoming trough to our northwest. \nDuring this transitional period, nocturnal convection is possible \neach night as moisture advection begins to intensify under dominant \nsouthwesterly flow aloft. As a result of this, eastern Utah and \nwestern Colorado will likely be waking each morning to cloud cover. \nThis cloud cover will linger throughout the day, leaving us with \nwidespread precipitation chances for the CWA this weekend. \nAdditionally, PWATs are expected to surge to ~150-190% of normal. As \nfar as temperatures are concerned, this weekend will see daytime \nhighs within 5 degrees of normal, and they continue to be on the \ndownward trend as we move towards next week. \n\nSATURDAY:\n\nStrong upper-level winds arriving Saturday will create enhanced wind \nshear along and north of I-70, setting the stage for a few strong to \nsevere thunderstorms. Damaging wind gusts will be the primary threat \nwith these storms. Gusts upwards of 60 mph were seen with some of \nFriday's storms. Therefore, cannot rule out a similar situation for \nSaturday, if not even higher gusts. Additionally, lightning, hail, \nand localized heavy downpours should not be ruled out. Flash \nflooding will also be a concern if storms slow down, stall, or \nrepeatedly track over the same area.  \n\nSUNDAY:\n\nScattered to widespread thunderstorms will redevelop on Sunday, but \nas the upper-level jet dynamics depart to the northeast, the primary \nthreat will shift from damaging wind to localized heavy rainfall. \nThis inherently enhances the risk for flash flooding and debris \nflows on recent burn scars and susceptible terrain.  \n\nNEXT WEEK:\n\nAs we begin next week, ensembles are supporting PWATs >150% of \nnormal through at least Monday, before a slight downturn in moisture \nfor Tuesday and Wednesday. Although, models begin to diverge in \noutcomes by Wednesday leading to rather low forecast confidence \nmidweek and beyond. All that being said, there still remains a \nsignal for another push of monsoonal moisture late next week. This \nmoisture could also be enhanced by a tropical system situated in the \neastern Pacific, which we will have to see evolve to gain a clearer \npicture. As for temperatures, a welcome relief is in store as \ndaytime highs are expected to fall below normal to start next week.",
